# Line endings: LF everywhere (the fix pack's owner ruling 2026-09-16, taken here under the
# 2026-09-17 standardisation). Git stores LF and
# checks text out as LF on every machine, whatever core.autocrlf says, because
# the eol attribute outranks it. The old CRLF checkout meant any tool that
# wrote LF left a file mixed, git could not see it, and a reader that split on
# the dominant ending silently lost lines. doccheck's EOL section is RED on a
# mixed file; the cure is `python tools/doccheck.py --fix-eol`.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.png binary
*.jpg binary

# Shell hooks must stay LF; a CRLF shebang dies silently on a fresh clone
# (`/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter`). Redundant with the line above, kept explicit.
tools/hooks/* text eol=lf
